            <ArticleTitle>Pyogenic liver abscess secondary to Streptococcus anginosus in an adolescent.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e15-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2010.02.044</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pyogenic liver abscess in the pediatric population is a rare entity and can be difficult to manage. Surgical infections caused by Streptococcus anginosus are exceedingly rare. Here we present a case of pyogenic liver abscess caused by S anginosus in an adolescent presenting with fever, nausea, emesis, and right upper quadrant abdominal discomfort.</AbstractText>
